language-derived-from-the-record-9 = unlines (cons (quoted (cons "**7. Etymology is in the meaning.** For a speaker, a word is the type of its occurrences in the speaker's record with the speaker's identifications as paths, and the present context selects the fiber of occurrences whose context is identified with it. A word's copying graph, the occurrences across speakers joined by copying, has earliest occurrences, for " (cons "law" (cons " the Old Norse lagu, what is laid down, and every later occurrence is joined to them by unbroken copying paths, the police included. When context selects the police occurrences, the paths to every other occurrence remain in the type: the fiber is cut out of the whole space, not out of its connections. So " (cons "the law" (cons " meaning the cops still carries what is laid down: the police are the laid-down, standing in the street. That is why the word lands as it does; a dictionary that numbers its senses cuts exactly these paths." nil)))))) nil)
